## Cryptographic Primitive Analysis

|Primitive                    |Used For                     |Strength|Quantum Resistance|Notes                              |
|-----------------------------|-----------------------------|--------|------------------|-----------------------------------|
|Scrypt (N=1M, r=8, p=1)      |PoW tokens, model hashing    |Strong  |No                |Memory-hard — ASIC-specific        |
|WireGuard (ChaCha20-Poly1305)|VPN tunnels                  |Strong  |No                |256-bit equivalent security        |
|TLS 1.3 (ECDHE-AES-256-GCM)  |HTTPS                        |Strong  |No                |Forward secrecy via ECDHE          |
|mTLS (same as TLS 1.3)       |Service-to-service           |Strong  |No                |Mutual authentication              |
|Ed25519                      |Agent signing, SSH, admin key|Strong  |No                |128-bit equivalent security        |
|LUKS2 (AES-XTS-256)          |Disk encryption              |Strong  |Partial           |AES-256 has some quantum resistance|
|rclone crypt (AES-256-CTR)   |Backup encryption            |Strong  |Partial           |                                   |
|WPA3-SAE (AES-256-CCMP)      |Wi-Fi uplink                 |Strong  |No                |                                   |
|DNSSEC                       |DNS integrity                |Strong  |No                |                                   |

**Post-quantum note:** None of the asymmetric primitives (Ed25519, ECDHE) are quantum-resistant. This is acceptable for a homelab context in 2026 but should be monitored as post-quantum standards mature. NIST post-quantum standards (CRYSTALS-Kyber, CRYSTALS-Dilithium) are candidates for future migration.

